findings.body:
The PR removes the XCTestExpectation and the `await fulfillment` from `testDownloadingSVGImage_withKingfisherProcessor_forStandardSVGCase`, so the test now returns right after it starts `downloadImage`. The completion handler, which holds all the `XCTAssertEqual` checks, runs asynchronously after the test has already finished. Those assertions can then never fail the test. The other tests in this file still use `wait(for:timeout:)`, so this one is out of step with them.

::diff{file="BrowserKit/Tests/SiteImageViewTests/SVGImageProcessorTests.swift" lines="12-37"}

::callout{type="warning"}
If `downloadImage` never calls back, or the callback fires late, this test still passes. It gives false confidence for the standard SVG path.

The PR deletes `@MainActor` from `startMockImageServer` together with the comment above it, which said GCDWebServer must be first initialized on the main thread and that the annotation prevents a crash when the file runs alone. The function still creates `GCDWebServer()` and starts it, but nothing now guarantees main-thread isolation. The tests were made synchronous, so the earlier `await` that forced an actor hop no longer exists.

::diff{file="BrowserKit/Tests/SiteImageViewTests/SVGImageProcessorTests.swift" lines="205-210"}

XCTest usually runs synchronous test methods on the main thread, so this may not crash in practice. But the documented safety requirement has been removed with no replacement. The better fix is to keep the guarantee on the test class or the helper.

The PR deletes the three-line Mozilla Public License header from the top of this file, but the other Swift sources in the same package keep it. Removing it also breaks the repo's license convention, which is probably enforced by lint.

::diff{file="BrowserKit/Sources/ExecutableContentBlockingGenerator/MainContentBlockerGenerator.swift" lines="1-4"}

The concurrency change in this file has nothing to do with the header, so the header should be restored.

`LabelButtonHeaderView.configure` now wraps the optional `moreButtonAction` in a new non-optional closure before passing it to `ActionButtonViewModel`. The wrapper is probably unnecessary. Swift can convert a `@Sendable @MainActor` function value to a plain `@MainActor` one, including inside an optional, so the parameter could be passed directly.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Home/Homepage/SectionHeader/LabelButtonHeaderView.swift" lines="103-113"}

The wrapper also changes `nil` into a non-nil no-op. `ActionButton` calls `touchUpAction?` so the behavior is the same, but the extra indirection makes the code harder to read. Verify that the direct pass compiles under Swift 6 and remove the wrapper.
